  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Dry Early with Precipitation Chances Saturday and \nSunday\n\nA trough continues to shift over the Great Lakes for today and \nFriday with a ridge building over the central CONUS. With northwest \nflow still in place and mid-level subsidence, no precipitation \nchances are expected through Friday. Temperatures also remain in the \nupper 70s to low 80s for both days. Heading into Saturday, while \nnorthwest flow aloft is still present, a shortwave shifts into our \nregion early Saturday morning as it rides the ridge increasing \nprecipitation chances. Another precipitation chance is possible on \nSunday as another wave rides the ridge. Moisture transport increases \nas 850mb winds shift to more southwesterly. This raises dewpoints \nback into the mid 60s to low 70s. Instability increases a little as \nMUCAPE values stay below 500 J/kg indicating that their could some \nthunderstorms along with the showers. Total rain amounts from \nSaturday are currently between 0.2 and 0.5\" with a 15 to 25% chance \nof more than 0.5\" across northwestern portions of the CWA. \nTemperatures continue to gradually warm up as we head into the \nweekend with highs getting into the mid to upper 80s for Sunday.\n\nMonday - Thursday: Warmer Temperatures and More Storm Chances \n\nThe ridge sets up to our south allowing our region to stay in \nsouthwest flow for much of the week. More waves look to pass through \naround the ridge and impact our region and with instability building \nin, there is potential for some strong to severe storms, however \nthat potential is low at the moment. AI and machine learning \nguidance do highlight a couple of days next week with a broad 5 to \n15% chance for severe weather due to increased instability and \nmodest shear values (30 to 40 kts of 0-6km shear). With the \nincreased storm potential (20 to 40% for any given day) next week, \nwhile NBM temperatures are forecasted in the mid to upper 80s, high \ntemperatures may be lower due to cloud cover and timing of \nprecipitation.",
